To retrieve the username from the JWT token in ASP.NET Core Web API, you can use the `JwtSecurityTokenHandler` class provided by the `Microsoft.IdentityModel.Tokens` namespace. Here's a step-by-step guide:

1. First, make sure you have installed the `Microsoft.IdentityModel.Tokens` package using NuGet.

2. In your ASP.NET Core Web API project, create a new method to extract the username from the JWT token. For example:

```csharp
using Microsoft.IdentityModel.Tokens;
using System.Security.Claims;

public string GetUsernameFromJwt(string jwtToken)
{
    var tokenHandler = new JwtSecurityTokenHandler();
    var key = Encoding.ASCII.Parse("your_secret_key"); // Replace this with your secret key
    var token = tokenHandler.ReadJwtToken(jwtToken, new TokenValidationParameters
    {
        IssuerSigningKey = key,
        ValidateIssuer = false,
        ValidateAudience = false,
        ValidateLifetime = false
    });

    var usernameClaim = token.Claims.FirstOrDefault(c => c.Type == "http://schemas.microsoft.com/identity/claims/username");
    if (usernameClaim != null)
    {
        return usernameClaim.Value;
    }
    else
    {
        return null;
    }
}
```

Replace `"your_secret_key"` with the actual secret key used to sign the JWT token.

3. Now, you can call this method in your controller action or any other part of your application where you need to access the username from the JWT token. For example:

```csharp
[HttpGet("api/users/{id}")]
public ActionResult<User> GetUser(int id, string jwtToken)
{
    var username = GetUsernameFromJwt(jwtToken);
    if (username == null)
    {
        return BadRequest("Invalid JWT token.");
    }

    // Retrieve the user with the given ID and username
    // ...
}
```

